            <ArticleTitle>Endoscopic partial laminectomy for cervical myelopathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>170-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECT" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The authors report the results of endoscopic partial laminectomy performed in 10 patients with degenerative cervical compressive myelopathy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Endoscopic partial laminectomy was performed safely in 10 patients with cervical myelopathy. All of the patients experienced symptomatic improvement with slight postoperative wound pain. The mean operative duration was 164+/-35 minutes and the mean intraoperative blood loss was 45.5+/-27 ml.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Endoscopic partial laminectomy may be used as a minimally invasive alternative for the treatment of cervical compressive myelopathy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
